 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance**: Significantly faster than recalculating the entire octree every frame by isolating dynamic updates [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Quality Trade-offs**: Using `InterlockedMax()` for normals reduces lighting quality for small voxel volumes [S1, unofficial]. Half-resolution cone tracing introduces up-scaling artifacts [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Limited cone tracing distance prevents sampling of distant objects (e.g., sky) [S1, unofficial]. The edge detection re-computation approach can still cause frame rate drops if the number of edge pixels is high [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced per-frame cost by only processing dynamic geometry and affected static nodes. Cone tracing is reduced by half-resolution processing, though edge repair adds overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** SVO structure requires extra space for dynamic buffer and 1-byte normal counter [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Faster than full re-voxelization. Edge repair at full resolution is costly; blurring is faster but lower quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes efficiently; reduces GPU load for indirect lighting.
+*   **Limitations:** Artifacts from `InterlockedMax()` on normals; artifacts from half-res upscaling; potential loss of far-field lighting [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method was described by Simon Brown in a blog post detailing the implementation of Voxel Cone Tracing. It represents a practical engineering optimization for real-time indirect illumination in dynamic scenes, published in January 2013 [S1, unofficial].
